{"text": " There are no kind of immediate shocks or surprises. I think it's in some respects, it's more of the same. It reflects the kind of glacial change that's kind of transpired in the last 20 to 30 years in relation to race equality and mobilising greater race equality in the UK. What we are seeing is that racism as a systemic and institutional problem has actually managed to reinvent itself. Its ability to continuously reinvent itself, re-collaborate itself and pivot to the pervading inequalities that exist is quite, in some respects, quite impressive in a strange way. The government would say that there's been huge amounts of progress. They did their own reports on race a couple of years ago where they said there isn't enough focus on the progress that has been made. Yeah, I think there have been some positives. It's important to recognise that. But by the same token, I don't think it's helpful to trivialise the kind of institutional and systemic nature of racism within British society. And I think it's so deeply interwoven within British society. I think trying to unpick those stitches by almost saying we're post-racial and actually we need to focus on the positives is actually a really sophisticated way of neutralising what is actually a very pernicious and pervasive problem within British society. Is there... Was there any value in that approach at all? When the report came out, it talked about too many communities sort of essentially clinging on to historical prejudices. No, because I think what the report kind of did in a very kind of sophisticated way was to place the blame almost on those communities. I think what it didn't do was actually think about, you know, how political inverences, you know, how the political structure in this country, how legislation within the British context has contributed largely to the kind of continual suffering of people of colour within Britain in terms of how they experience racism and how these kind of institutional instruments of the state continue to subordinate and disadvantage those groups. And there are many groups involved, aren't there? Yes, they're talking about black and minority ethnic groups, but travellers, for example, talk about a huge amount of racism, racial attacks towards them. If we want change, which we must, surely, how does that change come about? I think for me, the change comes about with us having, you know, secure policies that protect the rights of black ethnic minority traveller communities and ensure that actually there isn't infringements on their kind of civil liberties. What sorts of legislation on a day-to-day level would make a difference right now? I mean, the first thing would be to have an honest conversation about Britain's kind of uncomfortable past with acknowledging that Britain is an institutionally racist country. I think the second thing is then to recognise that, yes, while progress has been made, actually, this progress being sustained, a sustained effort over a number of years and a number of successive governments is a thing that we need to prioritise. And I think we need to have the right kind of agents around the kind of political table and around the political spectrum talking about these issues with really a collective way of thinking in terms of how we think about our societies and how we think about how we're actually going to truly dismantle institutional racism. Professor Jason Alday, thanks very much for coming in. Thank you. Well, in a statement, a government spokesperson said they're working towards a fairer society in Britain to make sectors like education, employment and criminal justice more inclusive.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 3.6, "text": " There are no kind of immediate shocks or surprises.", "tokens": [50363, 1318, 389, 645, 1611, 286, 7103, 32392, 393, 24072, 13, 50543], "temperature": 0, "avg_logprob": -0.18728017807006836, "compression_ratio": 1.6208333333333333, "no_speech_prob": 